  "configuration_id": "config_030",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best (config_024, acc=0.629) uses PCA for RNA encoding, and all multimodal experiments to date have used pca_n2000. The RNA encoder is the largest unexplored design dimension—PCA is a linear projection while scvi_n200 is a learned nonlinear representation that may capture biological structure better. Aggregation (avgpool > transformer) and fusion (concat ≈ weighted) are relatively well-characterized. Question: Does replacing PCA with a learned scvi encoder improve performance in the established best multimodal setup (multiprofile, avgpool, concat)? This experiment (config_030) is a clean one-variable comparison against config_018 (pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat, acc=0.619), isolating the RNA encoder effect. Why this is the best next step: It addresses the highest-uncertainty design choice with a direct A/B comparison, uses the well-validated ResNet50 backbone to avoid confounding with the ViT-L/F1 discrepancy seen in config_024, and if scvi helps, it opens a clear path to config_036 (scvi + ViT-L) as a follow-up; if it does not help, we save a more expensive ViT-L experiment and can pivot to other dimensions."
}

